## LAYER S — TEMPLATE SKILLS

Template id: landing
Selection source: filesystem by-template folder
Apply these skill manuals as template-specific craft guidance. They complement Layer B; they do not override Layer A platform rules, Layer B viewport mode, Layer E response format, or the output budget policy.

### Skill anti-ai-slop-ui-review
Source: by-template/landing/anti-ai-slop-ui-review.md

# Skill: Anti AI-Slop UI Review

Use this skill as a final design pass for any generated visual artifact.

## Core Directive

Remove the visual tells of generic AI output: flat hierarchy, default spacing, interchangeable cards,
weak typography, timid colors, decorative gradients without purpose, and identical section rhythm.

## Review Pass

- Identify the focal point of each major screen. If there is none, create one.
- Replace generic section titles with specific, audience-aware language.
- Break repetitive card grids with featured cards, asymmetry, grouping, or narrative rhythm.
- Increase contrast between primary and secondary content.
- Remove decorative elements that do not clarify mood, hierarchy, or interaction.
- Avoid default blue/purple SaaS palettes unless explicitly requested.
- Ensure motion has a reason: entrance, focus, feedback, or spatial orientation.

## Output Checks

- The artifact does not look like a default AI landing/dashboard template.
- The first viewport has a memorable composition.
- Typography choices feel intentional rather than browser-default.
- Every decorative layer supports a specific mood or message.

### Skill brand-led-identity-system
Source: by-template/landing/brand-led-identity-system.md

# Skill: Brand-Led Identity System

Use this skill when the artifact needs a strong, coherent visual identity across pages, slides,
posters, or campaigns.

## Core Directive

Create a small identity system inside the artifact. The output should feel like it belongs to one
brand, not like unrelated components assembled together.

## Identity Rules

- Define a recognizable palette with primary, secondary, neutral, and accent roles.
- Use one repeatable graphic motif: frame, badge, linework, symbol, pattern, crop, gradient, or grid.
- Keep typography consistent: display role, body role, label role, numeric/data role when needed.
- Repeat visual signatures across sections without making every block identical.
- Make image treatment consistent: crop ratio, mask shape, overlay style, border/radius, or caption system.
- Use logo/brand placeholder areas deliberately if no real brand asset exists.
- Make exports and screenshots look coherent even outside the full page context.

## Output Checks

- A section screenshot is still recognizable as part of the same artifact.
- The identity system can scale to another page or slide.
- Colors and type are roles, not isolated choices.
- The artifact has a visible brand point of view.

### Skill landing-conversion-copy
Source: by-template/landing/landing-conversion-copy.md

# landing-conversion-copy

Applies to: \`presetIds: landing\`, \`tags: conversion, cta, lead-gen\`

Use this skill when generating a conversion-oriented landing page.

## Goal

The artifact must guide one visitor segment toward one primary action. It is not a generic
company website.

## Required Structure

- Hero with offer, measurable promise, target audience, trust microcopy, and primary CTA.
- Problem section that names the user's pain, urgency, and cost of inaction.
- Solution section with 3-5 benefits phrased as outcomes.
- Proof section: testimonials, customer logos, metrics, guarantees, or credible placeholders.
- Offer/process section that explains what happens after the CTA.
- Objection-handling FAQ.
- Final CTA that repeats the same action.

## Copy Rules

- Keep the headline concrete and audience-specific.
- Use benefits before features.
- Every section must either increase desire, reduce risk, or clarify the next step.
- Use one primary CTA label consistently.

## Avoid

- Multiple competing CTAs.
- Generic "about us" sections that do not support conversion.
- Feature lists without user outcomes.
- Empty proof claims such as "trusted by many" without examples or placeholders.

## Acceptance Checks

- The primary CTA is visible above the fold.
- The page can be understood in 30 seconds.
- Each section supports the same conversion goal.
- FAQ answers at least price/time/risk/support or equivalent objections.

### Skill modern-impact-visual-direction
Source: by-template/landing/modern-impact-visual-direction.md

# Skill: Modern Impact Visual Direction

Use this skill when the artifact must feel current, intentional, and visually memorable rather
than like a generic generated page.

## Core Directive

Pick one strong visual direction and execute it consistently. Do not mix many weak motifs.
The output should have a clear point of view in typography, spacing, contrast, imagery, and motion.

## Required Decisions

- Define one dominant mood: editorial, cinematic, tactile, technical, playful, luxury, brutalist, or calm.
- Use a distinctive type scale: one expressive display treatment plus one readable body rhythm.
- Create a strong first screen with a focal object, asymmetric composition, or bold spatial tension.
- Use color with intent: one dominant background system, one accent family, and restrained neutrals.
- Avoid default SaaS sameness: no generic centered hero, no identical cards grid, no timid pastel accents.
- Use subtle atmospheric depth: gradients, grain, large shapes, oversized typography, image masks, or spatial layers.
- Add 1-3 meaningful animations only when they clarify hierarchy or create arrival energy.

## Output Checks

- The design is recognizable from a screenshot at 25 percent size.
- The top section has a clear visual anchor before reading the text.
- Cards, sections, and CTAs do not all share the same weight.
- The visual system could plausibly become a brand direction, not just a layout.

### Skill premium-landing-art-direction
Source: by-template/landing/premium-landing-art-direction.md

# Skill: Premium Landing Art Direction

Use this skill for landing pages that need conversion strength and high visual impact.

## Core Directive

Build the page as a persuasive product story, not a list of sections. Every block must answer a
specific visitor question and move them toward action.

## Landing Structure

- Hero: sharp promise, specific audience, one dominant CTA, one visual proof element.
- Problem tension: name the pain or missed opportunity in concrete language.
- Mechanism: explain how the product/service creates the outcome.
- Proof: metrics, use cases, testimonials, logos, screenshots, or before/after framing.
- Offer: package the value clearly, with what is included and why it matters now.
- Objection handling: address trust, cost, time, risk, setup, or support.
- Final CTA: repeat the strongest action with lower-friction language.

## Visual Direction

- Use a confident above-the-fold composition: split-stage, editorial poster, product cockpit, or cinematic panel.
- Give CTAs physical presence with contrast, spacing, and directional support.
- Use section transitions that feel designed: overlap, rhythm shift, color field, or narrative reveal.
- Use real-sounding copy and concrete nouns. Avoid generic claims like "innovative solution".

## Output Checks

- A visitor can understand the offer in 5 seconds.
- The page has one primary conversion path.
- At least three proof signals are visible before the final CTA.
- The design feels campaign-ready, not template-default.
